AXIS-512 Optimized Implementation

This directory contains the AXIS-512 implementation for mainstream 64-bit PC
software builds. It preserves the ICCS CryptHash API and can be built with the
included Makefile.

The current optimized code uses the 32-time-beat tape path in axis_core.c,
the CryptHash one-shot path for byte-aligned messages, and BMI2 PEXT message
packing when the selected optimization level permits it. AVX2 builds also
vectorize the 32-beat tap mix and compose/writeback work over the 8x32-bit
split structure-of-arrays state.

Optimization levels are selected at compile time with AXIS_OPT_LEVEL:
- 1: portable optimized, no AVX2/BMI2/native intrinsics.
- 2: AVX2 optimized, no BMI2/native tuning.
- 3: best available, enables native target tuning and BMI2 when available.

The Makefile exposes the same levels as `make portable`, `make avx2`, and
`make best`; plain `make` defaults to best available.
Byte-aligned one-shot hashing uses an AVX2 8x32-bit split structure-of-arrays
state so one vector covers all eight AXIS registers in the hot 32-beat block.
Long byte-aligned pure-message runs walk byte blocks with a descending pointer
so the hot absorb loop avoids repeated top-bit index arithmetic. The
long-message AVX2 paths keep the split state vectors in local registers across
the pure-message loop.
